<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03R3">Example <span data-tag="03R3">4.9.2.7</span> <span class="named">(Finite Cardinals)</span>.</a> Let $n$ be a nonnegative integer. Then a set $S$ has cardinality $n$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="03QX" href="/tag/03QX">4.9.2.1</a>) if and only if it has exactly $n$ elements: that is, there exists a bijection $S \simeq \{  0 &lt; 1 &lt; \cdots &lt; n-1 \} $. In particular, $n$ is a cardinal. We will say that a cardinal $\kappa $ is <em>finite</em> if it arises in this way (that is, if it is the cardinality of a finite set); otherwise, we say that $\kappa $ is <em>infinite</em>. </p>
